{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's own countercondition defeats its central claim in the context of the stated problem. The problem asks which 'published testing principles' support the proposed property. SRC_002 cites the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP), which is an object-oriented design heuristic about class cohesion, not a published testing principle governing test design and oracles. The countercondition 'If the system legitimately allows non-monotonic reordering or replacement... monotonicity alone is insufficient' concedes the asserted property is incomplete. The problem premise is merely that new evidence arrives; it does not restrict the system to append-only/shrink-only transitions, so a monotonicity assertion can fail to hold the general correctness property the problem demands, leaving SRC_002 without a valid published testing principle and with an inadequate proposed property.","counterexample":["A system whose legitimate evidence-admission contract permits replacing a superseded artifact with an amended one (a non-monotonic net-zero cardinality change). SRC_002's asserted monotonicity property would reject this legitimate transition, reproducing the exact fragility the problem asks to avoid, while yielding no recognized testing principle as support."],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 invokes Bertrand Meyer's Design by Contract and 'tests should verify behavioral contracts,' but it never states the general property the regression test should assert in the way the problem asks. Its proposed property is the 'class invariant of the artifact collection.' Its own countercondition concedes that if the invariant includes a cardinality constraint (e.g., 'exactly one canonical artifact per key'), an exact-count assertion may be the correct invariant. The problem's premise is that a fixed-count assertion fails when legitimate new evidence arrives; if the true invariant could itself be a fixed cardinality, then 'assert the invariant' does not prescribe the distinct general property (e.g., monotonic admission of valid evidence) the problem is asking for, and it supplies no published testing principle that rules out the exact-count form.","counterexample":["A collection whose class invariant genuinely states 'exactly one canonical artifact per key and all artifacts have unique IDs.' Under SRC_001's prescription, the test would assert that invariant, which may still entail a fixed count and fail on legitimate new evidence; the target gives no criterion distinguishing invariants that avoid the brittle-count failure from those that encode it."],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}